EXAM_DATA.papers["2018-english-one"] = {"id":"2018-english-one","year":2018,"label":"2018 英语一","era":"modern","sourceUrl":"https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/kaoyan/paper/2018-english-one/","timeLimitMin":180,"scoreMode":"estimated","parts":[{"id":"section1","kind":"cloze","title":"Section I 完形填空","directions":"Directions: Read the following text. Choose the best word(s) for each numbered blank and mark A, B, C or D on the ANSWER SHEET . (10 points)","points":10,"passage":{"paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section1-1","text":"Trust is a tricky business. On the one hand, it's a necessary condition   {{1}}   many worthwhile things: child care, friendships, etc. On the other hand, putting your   {{2}}   in the wrong place often carries a high   {{3}}   ."},{"id":"p-section1-2","text":"{{4}}   , why do we trust at all? Well, because it feels good.   {{5}}   people place their trust in an individual or an institution, their brains release oxytocin, a hormone that   {{6}}   pleasurable feelings and triggers the herding instinct that prompts humans to   {{7}}   with one another. Scientists have found that exposure   {{8}}   this hormone puts us in a trusting   {{9}}   : In a Swiss study, researchers sprayed oxytocin into the noses of half the subjects; those subjects were ready to lend significantly higher amounts of money to strangers than were their   {{10}}   who inhaled something else."},{"id":"p-section1-3","text":"{{11}}   for us, we also have a sixth sense for dishonesty that may   {{12}}   us. A Canadian study found that children as young as 14 months can differentiate   {{13}}   a credible person and a dishonest one. Sixty toddlers were each   {{14}}   to an adult tester holding a plastic container. The tester would ask, \"What's in here?\" before looking into the container, smiling, and exclaiming, \"Wow!\" Each subject was then invited to look   {{15}}   . Half of them found a toy; the other half   {{16}}   the container was empty – and realized the tester had   {{17}}   them."},{"id":"p-section1-4","text":"Among the children who had not been tricked, the majority were   {{18}}   to cooperate with the tester in learning a new skill, demonstrating that they trusted his leadership.   {{19}}   , only five of the 30 children paired with the \"   {{20}}   \" tester participated in a follow-up activity."}]},"questions":[{"n":1,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"on"},{"key":"B","text":"like"},{"key":"C","text":"for"},{"key":"D","text":"from"}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"固定搭配","stemZh":"1.","reason":"condition for sth = …的条件 线索就在本句内：a necessary condition __(1)__ many worthwhile things。condition 表\"做某事的条件\"时固定接 for，指向后面的\"那些有价值的事\"是信任所服务的对象。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"on 不与 condition 构成\"…的条件\"之义，on condition that 是\"以…为条件（从句）\"，结构和这里名词后接介词不同。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"like 意为\"像\"，condition like things 读不通，无此搭配。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"condition for sth 正是\"…的必要条件\"，for 把 condition 与其服务对象 many worthwhile things 接上，语法语义双合。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"from 表来源，与 condition 不搭，无 condition from 之说。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":2,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"faith"},{"key":"B","text":"concern"},{"key":"C","text":"attention"},{"key":"D","text":"interest"}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"2.","reason":"全文主题词 trust 是同义线索 本句 putting your __(2)__ in the wrong place often carries a high 是上句 Trust is a tricky business. 的延续，put faith in = trust（信任），空处要与 trust 同义，指\"把信任托付错了地方\"。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"put your faith in sth = 信任、信赖，正承接 Trust is a tricky business.，\"把信任放错地方\"，主题完全对位。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"concern 是\"关心/担忧\"，与\"信任\"主题不符，\"把担忧放错地方\"逻辑跑偏。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"attention 是\"注意力\"，pay attention 才搭，与 trust 无同义关系。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"interest 是\"兴趣、利益\"，放错兴趣讲不通，偏离信任话题。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":3,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"benefit"},{"key":"B","text":"debt"},{"key":"C","text":"hope"},{"key":"D","text":"price"}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"3.","reason":"wrong place 提示负面后果 线索在本句逻辑：putting your __(2)__ in the wrong place often carries a high __(3)__。\"放错地方\"是负面前提，carries a high ___ 应是\"付出高昂代价\"，high price（高代价）最契合。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"benefit 是\"好处\"，与\"放错地方\"的负面前提矛盾，\"代价高\"才合逻辑。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"debt 是具体的\"债务\"，carry a high debt 偏指金钱负债，无法概括信任错位带来的各种代价。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"hope 是\"希望\"，与负面后果无关，且 carry a high hope 不成立。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"carry/pay a high price 是\"付出高昂代价\"的固定说法，正承接\"信任放错地方\"的负面前提，褒贬与搭配双对。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":4,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Therefore"},{"key":"B","text":"Then"},{"key":"C","text":"Instead"},{"key":"D","text":"Again"}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"逻辑关系","stemZh":"4.","reason":"第1段陈述两面→第2段顺势发问 第1段讲信任有风险，第2段 __(4)__, why do we trust at all? 顺着\"既然有风险\"自然抛出疑问。这不是严格因果（不是\"所以\"），而是话题推进式的\"那么\"。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"Therefore 表\"因此\"，需前文是明确原因、后文是结论；这里后文是疑问句，不构成因果结论，逻辑过重。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"Then = \"那么\"，承上文\"信任既有益又有险\"顺势发问 why do we trust at all?，是引出新问题的自然过渡。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"Instead 表\"反而、相反\"，前后并无对立替换关系，逻辑不成立。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"Again 表\"再次\"，前文未问过同一问题，谈不上\"再问一遍\"。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":5,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Until"},{"key":"B","text":"Unless"},{"key":"C","text":"Although"},{"key":"D","text":"When"}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"逻辑关系","stemZh":"5.","reason":"\"信任时\"伴随\"分泌催产素\" 线索在主从句的同步关系：__(5)__ people place their trust in an individual or an institution, their brains release oxytocin——人一旦信任，大脑就释放催产素，是\"当…时\"的伴随，非条件也非转折。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"Until 表\"直到…才\"，意为分泌催产素发生在信任之前为止，与\"信任时即分泌\"的同步关系不符。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"Unless = \"除非（不）\"，会变成\"除非不信任才分泌\"，逻辑完全颠倒。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"Although 表让步\"尽管\"，但信任与分泌催产素是顺承因果，并无对立可让步。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"When = \"当…时\"，准确表达\"人一信任，大脑便释放催产素\"的同步发生，与下文机制描述一致。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":6,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"selects"},{"key":"B","text":"produces"},{"key":"C","text":"applies"},{"key":"D","text":"maintains"}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"6.","reason":"激素\"产生\"快感并\"引发\"本能 线索在并列结构：a hormone that __(6)__ pleasurable feelings and triggers the herding instinct。与 triggers（引发）并列，空处也应是\"产生/带来\"快感这一正向制造义。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"selects 是\"选择\"，激素\"选择\"快感讲不通。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"produces = \"产生、带来\"，激素 produces pleasurable feelings，与并列的 triggers 同向，准确表达\"催产素让人愉悦\"。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"applies 是\"应用、涂抹\"，apply feelings 不成立。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"maintains 是\"维持\"，强调保持已有状态，而文意是催产素\"制造\"出愉悦感，不是维持原有的。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":7,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"consult"},{"key":"B","text":"compete"},{"key":"C","text":"connect"},{"key":"D","text":"compare"}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"7.","reason":"herding instinct 指向\"联结\" 线索在本句：triggers the herding instinct that prompts humans to __(7)__ with one another。群居本能促使人\"彼此联结/抱团\"，方向是亲近合作，不是竞争或比较。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"consult 是\"咨询、商议\"，与\"群居本能\"的\"抱团\"语义不正对。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"compete = \"竞争\"，与\"群居、合群\"的合作色彩相反。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"connect = \"联结、与…亲近\"，connect with one another 正是群居本能驱使下\"彼此联系抱团\"，色彩与 herding instinct 一致。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"compare = \"比较\"，与群居合群无关，逻辑跑偏。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":8,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"at"},{"key":"B","text":"by"},{"key":"C","text":"of"},{"key":"D","text":"to"}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"固定搭配","stemZh":"8.","reason":"exposure to sth = 接触某物 线索在本句搭配：exposure __(8)__ this hormone puts us in a trusting。exposure to = \"接触、暴露于\"，指\"接触这种激素\"，是固定介词搭配。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"at 不与 exposure 搭配。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"by 与 exposure 不构成\"接触\"义。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"of 虽常接名词，但 exposure of 多指\"…的曝光/揭露\"，与此处\"接触激素\"义不符。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"to 构成 exposure to this hormone\"接触这种激素\"，是 expose 系列的固定介词，语法语义双合。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":9,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"context"},{"key":"B","text":"mood"},{"key":"C","text":"period"},{"key":"D","text":"circle"}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"9.","reason":"下文实验证明是\"心境\"变化 线索跨到下句：those subjects were ready to lend significantly higher amounts of money——接触激素后人\"更愿意借钱\"，说明催产素改变的是一种\"信任的心境/情绪\"，trusting mood（信任心境）最贴。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"context 是\"上下文、背景环境\"，是外部条件，而催产素作用于人内部的心理状态，对象错位。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"mood = \"心境、情绪状态\"，a trusting mood\"信任的心境\"，正是催产素作用于人内心、令其更愿借钱的结果。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"period 是\"时期\"，强调时间段，与\"心理状态\"无关。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"circle 是\"圈子\"，指人群范围，与\"被激素带入的心境\"不符。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":10,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"counterparts"},{"key":"B","text":"substitutes"},{"key":"C","text":"colleagues"},{"key":"D","text":"supporters"}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"10.","reason":"实验对照组——吸入别物的另一半 线索在对比句：those subjects were ready to lend significantly higher amounts of money to strangers than were their __(10)__ who inhaled something else。前组吸催产素、后组 inhaled something else，二者是同一实验里地位对应的两组人，即\"对照者/对应者\"。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"counterparts = \"对应的人、相对照者\"，正指实验中与受试组地位对等、吸入别物的对照组，与对比结构完全吻合。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"substitutes 是\"替补、替代品\"，两组人是并列对照、非替代关系。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"colleagues = \"同事\"，受试者之间无同事关系，无中生有。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"supporters = \"支持者\"，与实验对照组身份无关。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":11,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Funny"},{"key":"B","text":"Lucky"},{"key":"C","text":"Odd"},{"key":"D","text":"Ironic"}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"逻辑关系","stemZh":"11.","reason":"\"有识谎第六感\"是好事 线索在本句内容：__(11)__ for us, we also have a sixth sense for dishonesty that may __(12)__ us——人还有识别欺骗的第六感、能保护自己，对我们是\"幸运\"的好事，故用褒义评价。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"Funny 是\"好笑、古怪\"，识谎能力是实在的好处，不是\"好笑\"。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"Lucky = \"幸运的是\"，Lucky for us 正评价\"我们还有识谎第六感能自保\"这桩好事，褒贬与后文一致。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"Odd = \"奇怪的\"，识谎是正常进化优势，并不\"奇怪\"。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"Ironic = \"讽刺的是\"，需事与愿违的反差；\"有保护机制\"顺理成章，无讽刺可言。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":12,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"monitor"},{"key":"B","text":"protect"},{"key":"C","text":"surprise"},{"key":"D","text":"delight"}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"12.","reason":"识谎能力的作用=保护 线索在本句逻辑与第11空呼应：we also have a sixth sense for dishonesty that may __(12)__ us，既然第11空已定\"Lucky for us（幸运）\"，这第六感对人的作用就是\"protect us（保护我们）\"免受欺骗。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"monitor = \"监控\"，第六感是\"监控我们\"讲不通，对象关系反了。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"protect = \"保护\"，识谎第六感 protect us（保护我们免遭欺骗），与 __(11)__ for us 的\"幸运\"评价完全一致。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"surprise = \"使惊讶\"，第六感\"使我们惊讶\"与\"幸运、自保\"逻辑不接。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"delight = \"使高兴\"，第六感的核心作用是防骗自保，而非令人愉悦。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":13,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"between"},{"key":"B","text":"within"},{"key":"C","text":"toward"},{"key":"D","text":"over"}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"固定搭配","stemZh":"13.","reason":"differentiate between A and B 线索在本句结构：differentiate __(13)__ a credible person and a dishonest one——\"在可信者与不诚实者之间作区分\"，后跟 A and B 两个并列对象，正是 between 的典型框架。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"differentiate between A and B = \"区分A和B\"，between 与后文 a credible person and a dishonest one 的两者结构精确匹配。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"within 表\"在…之内\"，differentiate within 不成立，且\"两者之间\"非\"之内\"。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"toward 表方向\"朝向\"，不与 differentiate 搭配。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"over 与 differentiate 无搭配关系。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":14,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"transferred"},{"key":"B","text":"added"},{"key":"C","text":"introduced"},{"key":"D","text":"entrusted"}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"14.","reason":"实验里把幼儿\"引见\"给测试者 线索在本句场景：Sixty toddlers were each __(14)__ to an adult tester holding a plastic container——把每个幼儿\"引见/带到\"一位成人测试者面前开始实验，introduce sb to sb 正是\"把某人引见给某人\"。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"transferred = \"转移、调动\"，把孩子\"转移给\"测试者像搬物件，不合\"引见见面\"语境。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"added = \"添加\"，add toddlers to a tester 不通。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"introduced = \"引见、介绍给\"，be introduced to an adult tester\"被引见给一位成人测试者\"，准确描述实验起始的接触环节。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"entrusted = \"委托、托付\"，entrust sb to sb 含\"把人交托照管\"，过重且不符\"见面做实验\"场景。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":15,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"out"},{"key":"B","text":"back"},{"key":"C","text":"around"},{"key":"D","text":"inside"}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"15.","reason":"下文\"找到玩具/容器是空的\"指向往里看 线索在后句结果：Half of them found a toy; the other half __(16)__ the container was empty——孩子要发现容器里\"有玩具/是空的\"，必须 look inside（往里看），故选 inside。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"out = \"向外\"，look out 是\"往外看/当心\"，与\"看容器内部\"方向相反。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"back = \"往回看\"，与\"看容器里\"无关。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"around = \"四处看\"，孩子是定点看容器内部，非环顾四周。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"inside = \"往里面（看）\"，look inside 正对应下文\"发现里面有玩具或是空的\"，方向精确。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":16,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"discovered"},{"key":"B","text":"proved"},{"key":"C","text":"insisted"},{"key":"D","text":"remembered"}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"16.","reason":"与前半 found a toy 平行 线索在并列对照：Half of them found a toy; the other half __(16)__ the container was empty——前半\"发现一个玩具\"，后半应对称地\"发现容器是空的\"，discover 与 found 同义平行。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"discovered = \"发现\"，与前半 found a toy 平行对称，\"另一半发现容器是空的\"，语义结构双合。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"proved = \"证明\"，孩子只是看见、不是\"证明\"容器空，语义过重。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"insisted = \"坚持认为\"，孩子是亲眼所见的事实，无需\"坚称\"。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"remembered = \"记得\"，强调回忆既有信息，而此处是当场往里看\"发现\"新情况。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":17,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"betrayed"},{"key":"B","text":"wronged"},{"key":"C","text":"fooled"},{"key":"D","text":"mocked"}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"17.","reason":"假装有物=骗了孩子 线索在情节与第13空呼应：测试者看进空容器却 exclaiming, \"Wow!\"，孩子却 realized the tester had __(17)__ them——这正是\"耍弄、欺骗\"了孩子，与全段\"a dishonest one（骗子）\"主题对应，fooled（愚弄）最贴。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"betrayed = \"背叛\"，需先有信任与忠诚关系被辜负，对陌生测试者一次小把戏而言过重。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"wronged = \"冤枉、不公正对待\"，测试者是\"骗\"不是\"冤枉\"孩子。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"fooled = \"愚弄、欺骗\"，准确描述\"假装空容器里有东西\"这一把戏，呼应第3段 dishonesty 主题。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"mocked = \"嘲笑、戏弄\"，重在\"取笑\"，而测试者是\"骗\"非\"笑\"孩子。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":18,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"forced"},{"key":"B","text":"willing"},{"key":"C","text":"hesitant"},{"key":"D","text":"entitled"}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"18.","reason":"信任→愿意配合 线索在本句因果：the majority were __(18)__ to cooperate with the tester ..., demonstrating that they trusted his leadership——既然\"信任其领导\"，多数孩子就\"愿意\"配合，willing（愿意的）顺理成章。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"forced = \"被迫\"，与\"信任他、主动配合\"矛盾，被迫不能体现\"信任其领导\"。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"willing = \"愿意的\"，be willing to cooperate\"愿意配合\"，正由\"信任其领导\"导出，褒贬逻辑双合。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"hesitant = \"犹豫的\"，与\"信任、主动配合\"相悖。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"entitled = \"有资格的\"，be entitled to 表\"有权做\"，与\"愿不愿意配合\"的态度无关。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":19,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"In contrast"},{"key":"B","text":"As a result"},{"key":"C","text":"On the whole"},{"key":"D","text":"For instance"}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"逻辑关系","stemZh":"19.","reason":"多数配合 vs 仅5人参与=对比 线索在前后两句的反差：前句 the majority were __(18)__ to cooperate（未被骗组多数配合），本句 only five of the 30 children paired with the \"__(20)__\" tester participated（被骗组仅5人参与），是鲜明对比，故用 In contrast。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"In contrast = \"相比之下\"，精确连接\"未被骗组多数配合\"与\"被骗组仅5人参与\"的相反结果，对比逻辑成立。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"As a result = \"结果\"，两组是并列对照的两种情况，后句不是前句的因果结果。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"On the whole = \"总的来说\"，用于总结归纳，而此处是两组对比、非总结。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"For instance = \"例如\"，后句不是对前句的举例，而是相反一组的结果。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":20,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"inflexible"},{"key":"B","text":"incapable"},{"key":"C","text":"unreliable"},{"key":"D","text":"unsuitable"}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"20.","reason":"对应前文\"骗孩子的不诚实者\" 线索回指第3段：测试者 had __(17)__ them（骗了孩子），与 a credible person and a dishonest one 中的\"dishonest one\"对应。骗子=不可信赖，故引号里贴的标签是 unreliable（不可靠的）。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"inflexible = \"僵化、不灵活\"，测试者的问题是\"骗人\"而非\"死板\"。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"incapable = \"无能的\"，测试者不是\"没能力\"，而是\"不诚实\"。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"unreliable = \"不可靠、不可信的\"，正概括\"骗了孩子\"的测试者，与第3段 dishonest one 同义对应。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"unsuitable = \"不合适的\"，泛指不适宜，未点出\"不诚实/不可信\"这一核心。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}}]},{"id":"section2-part-a-1","kind":"reading","title":"Text 1","textNo":1,"directions":"Directions: Read the following four texts. Answer the questions after each text by choosing A, B, C or D. Mark your answers on the ANSWER SHEET . (40 points)","points":10,"passage":{"title":"Text 1","paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section2-part-a-1-1","text":"Among the annoying challenges facing the middle class is one that will probably go unmentioned in the next presidential campaign: What happens when the robots come for their jobs?"},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-1-2","text":"Don't dismiss that possibility entirely. About half of U.S. jobs are at high risk of being automated, according to a University of Oxford study, with the middle class disproportionately squeezed. Lower-income jobs like gardening or day care don't appeal to robots. But many middle-class occupations – trucking, financial advice, software engineering – have aroused their interest, or soon will. The rich own the robots, so they will be fine."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-1-3","text":"This isn't to be alarmist. Optimists point out that technological upheaval has benefited workers in the past. The Industrial Revolution didn't go so well for Luddites whose jobs were displaced by mechanized looms, but it eventually raised living standards and created more jobs than it destroyed. Likewise, automation should eventually boost productivity, stimulate demand by driving down prices, and free workers from hard, boring work. But in the medium term, middle-class workers may need a lot of help adjusting."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-1-4","text":"The first step, as Erik Brynjolfsson and Andrew McAfee argue in The Second Machine Age, should be rethinking education and job training. Curriculums – from grammar school to college – should evolve to focus less on memorizing facts and more on creativity and complex communication. Vocational schools should do a better job of fostering problem-solving skills and helping students work alongside robots. Online education can supplement the traditional kind. It could make extra training and instruction affordable. Professionals trying to acquire new skills will be able to do so without going into debt."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-1-5","text":"The challenge of coping with automation underlines the need for the U.S. to revive its fading business dynamism: Starting new companies must be made easier. In previous eras of drastic technological change, entrepreneurs smoothed the transition by dreaming up ways to combine labor and machines. The best uses of 3D printers and virtual reality haven't been invented yet. The U.S. needs the new companies that will invent them."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-1-6","text":"Finally, because automation threatens to widen the gap between capital income and labor income, taxes and the safety net will have to be rethought. Taxes on low-wage labor need to be cut, and wage subsidies such as the earned income tax credit should be expanded: This would boost incomes, encourage work, reward companies for job creation, and reduce inequality."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-1-7","text":"Technology will improve society in ways big and small over the next few years, yet this will be little comfort to those who find their lives and careers upended by automation. Destroying the machines that are coming for our jobs would be nuts. But policies to help workers adapt will be indispensable."}]},"questions":[{"n":21,"stem":"Who will be most threatened by automation?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Leading politicians."},{"key":"B","text":"Low-wage laborers."},{"key":"C","text":"Robot owners."},{"key":"D","text":"Middle-class workers."}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"细节题","stemZh":"谁将受到自动化最大的威胁?","choicesZh":{"A":"主要政治家。","B":"低薪劳动者。","C":"机器人所有者。","D":"中产阶级工人。"},"analysis":{"type":"Who will be most threatened by automation? 直接问\"谁\"，答案是文本里写明的具体群体，无须推理。","parse":"Who will be most threatened by automation? 主干是 Who will be threatened，most 修饰过去分词作表语，by automation 是被动施动者。结构平铺直叙、无陷阱。","locateEn":"About half of U.S. jobs are at high risk of being automated, according to a University of Oxford study, with the middle class disproportionately squeezed. Lower-income jobs like gardening or day care don't appeal to robots. But many middle-class occupations – trucking, financial advice, software engineering – have aroused their interest, or soon will. The rich own the robots, so they will be fine.","locateZh":"据牛津大学一项研究，约半数美国岗位有被自动化取代的高风险，而中产阶级被不成比例地挤压。园艺、日托这类低收入工作机器人看不上；但卡车驾驶、理财顾问、软件工程等许多中产职业已经吸引了它们的兴趣，或很快会。富人拥有机器人，所以富人没事。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"What happens when the robots come for their jobs? 提到 the next presidential campaign，是说政客不会谈这个话题，并非政客本身受自动化威胁。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"原文 Lower-income jobs like gardening or day care don't appeal to robots 显式说明低收入工作机器人不感兴趣，恰恰\"不\"受威胁；选项与原文反向。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"原文 The rich own the robots, so they will be fine 显式说明机器人所有者\"没事\"，与\"受威胁\"反向。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"预判\"中产最受威胁\"与选项 Middle-class workers 字面对应；A/B/C 三项与原文显式陈述相反或无关。","pairs":[{"orig":"the middle class disproportionately squeezed","opt":"Middle-class workers"},{"orig":"many middle-class occupations – trucking, financial advice, software engineering – have aroused their interest","opt":"Middle-class workers"}]}}}}},{"n":22,"stem":"Which of the following best represents the author's view?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Worries about automation are in fact groundless."},{"key":"B","text":"Optimists' opinions on new tech find little support."},{"key":"C","text":"Issues arising from automation need to be tackled."},{"key":"D","text":"Negative consequences of new tech can be avoided."}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"主旨题","stemZh":"以下哪项最能代表作者的观点?","choicesZh":{"A":"对自动化的担忧实际上毫无根据。","B":"乐观主义者对新技术的看法几乎得不到支持。","C":"自动化引发的问题需要得到解决。","D":"新技术的负面后果是可以避免的。"},"analysis":{"type":"best represents the author's view 求的是覆盖全文的态度/观点，而不是某一段细节。","parse":"Which of the following best represents the author's view? Which 引导特殊疑问，best 修饰动词 represents，author's view 作宾语。结构平铺直叙、无陷阱。","locateEn":"","locateZh":"","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"A 项 Worries about automation are in fact groundless 说担忧毫无根据；但作者在 P2 明列\"中产被挤压\"、在 P3 用 But 强调\"中期内需帮助\"、在 P7 称政策\"不可或缺\"，全文都在确认担忧有据。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"原文 Optimists point out that technological upheaval has benefited workers in the past 与 Likewise, automation should eventually boost productivity, stimulate demand by driving down prices, and free workers from hard, boring work 实际上认可乐观派的长期判断；作者只反对\"中期内不需帮助\"，并未说乐观观点\"几乎没有支持\"。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"C 项 Issues arising from automation need to be tackled 既承认 Issues（问题真实），又提出 need to be tackled（须解决），与预判两层完全契合。","pairs":[{"orig":"But in the medium term, middle-class workers may need a lot of help adjusting","opt":"Issues arising from automation need to be tackled"},{"orig":"But policies to help workers adapt will be indispensable","opt":"Issues arising from automation need to be tackled"}]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"原文 middle-class workers may need a lot of help adjusting 和 policies to help workers adapt will be indispensable 都是\"帮助适应\"，不是\"完全避免\"；this will be little comfort to those who find their lives and careers upended by automation 反而承认\"被自动化颠覆的人很难被安慰\"，负面后果客观存在。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":23,"stem":"Education in the age of automation should put more emphasis on____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"creative potential."},{"key":"B","text":"job-hunting skills."},{"key":"C","text":"individual needs."},{"key":"D","text":"cooperative spirit."}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"细节题","stemZh":"自动化时代的教育应更加强调____","choicesZh":{"A":"创造潜力。","B":"求职技能。","C":"个人需求。","D":"合作精神。"},"analysis":{"type":"Education in the age of automation should put more emphasis on____ 是补全句，填的是教育应强调的具体内容，须回原文取词。","parse":"Education in the age of automation should put more emphasis on____ 主语 Education 带后置介词短语修饰，谓语 should put、宾语 more emphasis，on 后接的空位待填。结构平铺直叙、无陷阱。","locateEn":"Curriculums – from grammar school to college – should evolve to focus less on memorizing facts and more on creativity and complex communication.","locateZh":"从小学到大学的课程应当演变为少记忆事实、多侧重创造力和复杂沟通。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"四个选项中只有 A 含 creative 词根，与定位句 more on 后的 creativity 直接对应；其余三项均不在 more on 的指向范围内。","pairs":[{"orig":"creativity and complex communication","opt":"creative potential"}]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"本段提到 fostering problem-solving skills 与 helping students work alongside robots，但都不是\"求职技能\"；原文未出现 job-hunting 概念。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"Online education can supplement the traditional kind. It could make extra training and instruction affordable 谈的是\"价格可负担\"，不是\"针对个体差异\"；individual needs 在原文无对应。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"complex communication 与 work alongside robots 都隐含\"协同\"，但不等于\"合作精神\"作为教育的强调重点；原文 more on 后只列出 creativity 与 complex communication，未把\"合作精神\"作为单独强调项。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":24,"stem":"The author suggests that tax policies be aimed at____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"encouraging the development of automation."},{"key":"B","text":"increasing the return on capital investment."},{"key":"C","text":"easing the hostility between rich and poor."},{"key":"D","text":"preventing the income gap from widening."}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"细节题","stemZh":"作者建议税收政策应旨在____","choicesZh":{"A":"鼓励自动化的发展。","B":"提高资本投资的回报。","C":"缓解贫富之间的敌意。","D":"防止收入差距扩大。"},"analysis":{"type":"The author suggests that tax policies be aimed at____ 是补全句，填的是税收政策的具体目的，可在原文中找到对应陈述。","parse":"The author suggests that tax policies be aimed at____ suggest 引导虚拟语气从句，从句省略 should，谓语用动词原形 be aimed at。注意 suggest \"建议\"用法下从句动词原形是固定考点，不是病句。","locateEn":"Finally, because automation threatens to widen the gap between capital income and labor income, taxes and the safety net will have to be rethought.","locateZh":"最后，由于自动化有扩大资本收入与劳动收入差距的危险，税收和社会保障必须被重新思考。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"原文政策目的是\"应对\"自动化带来的负面收入分化，不是\"鼓励\"自动化本身；本段从头到尾没有\"促进自动化\"的字样。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"原文 Taxes on low-wage labor need to be cut, and wage subsidies such as the earned income tax credit should be expanded 减税与补贴的对象是\"低薪劳动\"，目的是提高劳动者收入；不是提高\"资本投资回报\"，恰是相反方向。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"段末 boost incomes, encourage work, reward companies for job creation, and reduce inequality 列的是\"减不平等\"，不是\"缓解贫富之间的敌意\"；hostility（敌意）是情感层面，原文谈的是物质收入层面，不等价。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"预判\"防止差距扩大\"与 D 项 preventing the income gap from widening 字面对应；A/B/C 三项都偏离\"防止收入差距扩大\"这一目的。","pairs":[{"orig":"because automation threatens to widen the gap between capital income and labor income","opt":"preventing the income gap from widening"}]}}}}},{"n":25,"stem":"In this text, the author presents a problem with____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"opposing views on it."},{"key":"B","text":"possible solutions to it."},{"key":"C","text":"its alarming impacts."},{"key":"D","text":"its major variations."}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"主旨题","stemZh":"在本文中,作者提出了一个问题及其____","choicesZh":{"A":"相反的观点。","B":"可能的解决方案。","C":"令人担忧的影响。","D":"主要变化。"},"analysis":{"type":"In this text, the author presents a problem with____ 求的是\"作者用什么方式呈现问题\"，覆盖全文论述结构。","parse":"In this text, the author presents a problem with____ 主干是 the author presents a problem，with 后空位待填修饰 problem 的方式。结构平铺直叙、无陷阱。","locateEn":"","locateZh":"","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"原文仅在 P3 提及 Optimists 一处他人观点，作者随即用 But 收束并转入方案，全文主体是\"方案陈述\"而非\"观点对立\"；选项 opposing views on it 把次要环节当主结构，与文章重心错位。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"预判\"提出问题+给出多种解决方案\"与 B 项 possible solutions to it 完美对应；it 指首段提出的 problem，solutions 复数正对应文中三项并列方案。","pairs":[{"orig":"The first step, as Erik Brynjolfsson and Andrew McAfee argue in The Second Machine Age, should be rethinking education and job training","opt":"possible solutions to it"},{"orig":"Finally, because automation threatens to widen the gap between capital income and labor income, taxes and the safety net will have to be rethought","opt":"possible solutions to it"}]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"作者在 P3 明确说 This isn't to be alarmist（这不是危言耸听）以自我克制；alarming impacts（惊人冲击）不是本文的叙述基调。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"原文未对\"自动化\"分类（如工业自动化、办公自动化、AI 等）也未对其表现形式做分门别类的讨论；major variations（主要变种）在原文无对应。","pairs":[]}}}}}]},{"id":"section2-part-a-2","kind":"reading","title":"Text 2","textNo":2,"directions":"","points":10,"passage":{"title":"Text 2","paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section2-part-a-2-1","text":"A new survey by Harvard University finds more than two-thirds of young Americans disapprove of President Trump's use of Twitter. The implication is that Millennials prefer news from the White House to be filtered through other sources, not a president's social media platform."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-2-2","text":"Most Americans rely on social media to check daily headlines. Yet as distrust has risen toward all media, people may be starting to beef up their media literacy skills. Such a trend is badly needed. During the 2016 presidential campaign, nearly a quarter of web content shared by Twitter users in the politically critical state of Michigan was fake news, according to the University of Oxford. And a survey conducted for BuzzFeed News found 44 percent of Facebook users rarely or never trust news from the media giant."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-2-3","text":"Young people who are digital natives are indeed becoming more skillful at separating fact from fiction in cyberspace. A Knight Foundation focus-group survey of young people between ages 14 and 24 found they use \"distributed trust\" to verify stories. They cross-check sources and prefer news from different perspectives – especially those that are open about any bias. \"Many young people assume a great deal of personal responsibility for educating themselves and actively seeking out opposing viewpoints,\" the survey concluded."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-2-4","text":"Such active research can have another effect. A 2014 survey conducted in Australia, Britain, and the United States by the University of Wisconsin-Madison found that young people's reliance on social media led to greater political engagement."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-2-5","text":"Social media allows users to experience news events more intimately and immediately while also permitting them to re-share news as a projection of their values and interests. This forces users to be more conscious of their role in passing along information. A survey by Barna research group found the top reason given by Americans for the fake news phenomenon is \"reader error,\" more so than made-up stories or factual mistakes in reporting. About a third say the problem of fake news lies in \"misinterpretation or exaggeration of actual news\" via social media. In other words, the choice to share news on social media may be the heart of the issue. \"This indicates there is a real personal responsibility in counteracting this problem,\" says Roxanne Stone, editor in chief at Barna Group."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-2-6","text":"So when young people are critical of an over-tweeting president, they reveal a mental discipline in thinking skills – and in their choices on when to share on social media."}]},"questions":[{"n":26,"stem":"According to Paragraphs 1 and 2, many young Americans cast doubts on____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"the justification of the news-filtering practice."},{"key":"B","text":"people's preference for social media platforms."},{"key":"C","text":"the administration's ability to handle information."},{"key":"D","text":"social media as a reliable source of news."}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"细节题","stemZh":"根据第1段和第2段,许多美国年轻人对____表示怀疑","choicesZh":{"A":"新闻过滤做法的正当性","B":"人们对社交媒体平台的偏好","C":"政府处理信息的能力","D":"社交媒体作为可靠新闻来源的地位"},"analysis":{"type":"题干 According to Paragraphs 1 and 2 后接一个待补全的陈述句，问\"年轻人怀疑什么\"，是对特定段落事实信息的提取。","parse":"题干主干 young Americans cast doubts on____，cast doubts on 是固定搭配（对……表示怀疑），空格在介词 on 之后，要填名词性成分。无从句、无否定，结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。","locateEn":"The implication is that Millennials prefer news from the White House to be filtered through other sources, not a president's social media platform.","locateZh":"其言下之意是：千禧一代更愿意白宫的新闻经由其他渠道过滤呈现，而不是来自总统的社交媒体平台。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"原文 prefer news...to be filtered through other sources 是年轻人本人偏好的做法，他们怀疑的是社媒，不是\"过滤本身\"；选项把年轻人推崇的方式说成被怀疑的对象，方向反了。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"题干问\"年轻人怀疑什么\"，而本项的 people's preference for social media platforms 是把\"人们偏好社媒\"当作怀疑对象——原文从未否定\"人们偏好社媒\"这件事，否定的只是把社媒当新闻可信源。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"原文从未质疑政府\"处理信息的能力\"；P1 说的是年轻人反对总统使用 Twitter 这一渠道，与\"行政能力\"无关。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"预判的核心\"怀疑社媒作新闻来源\"与 social media as a reliable source of news 字面对位，是定位句两端意思的最干净改写。","pairs":[{"orig":"Millennials prefer news from the White House to be filtered through other sources, not a president's social media platform","opt":"social media as a reliable source of news"},{"orig":"as distrust has risen toward all media","opt":"social media as a reliable source of news"}]}}}}},{"n":27,"stem":"The phrase \"beef up\" (Line 2, Para. 2) is closest in meaning to____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"sharpen."},{"key":"B","text":"define."},{"key":"C","text":"boast."},{"key":"D","text":"share."}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"词义句意题","stemZh":"短语\"beef up\"(第2段第2行)在意思上最接近____","choicesZh":{"A":"加强","B":"界定","C":"吹嘘","D":"分享"},"analysis":{"type":"题干 The phrase \"beef up\" (Line 2, Para. 2) is closest in meaning to____ 直接问\"最接近的含义\"，是典型的词义题问法。","parse":"The phrase \"beef up\" (Line 2, Para. 2) is closest in meaning to____ 主系表结构，主语是短语本身、表语是空格处的同义词。无陷阱。","locateEn":"Yet as distrust has risen toward all media, people may be starting to beef up their media literacy skills.","locateZh":"然而随着对所有媒体的不信任上升，人们或许正开始强化自身的媒介素养能力。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"在\"增强能力\"这个语义场里，sharpen 是唯一能与 skills 自然搭配且极性一致的选项，与预判完美对位。","pairs":[{"orig":"beef up their media literacy skills","opt":"sharpen"}]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"define 意为\"给……下定义/明确界定\"，原文语境是\"增强能力\"，与 define 的语义场不重合。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"boast 意为\"自夸、夸耀拥有\"，带轻微负面或炫耀色彩，与原文\"理性提升能力\"的中性/正面极性不符。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"share 意为\"分享\"，与 skills 不构成\"增强\"的搭配；且原文\"分享\"另有其词（re-share），分布在 P5，不在本句。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":28,"stem":"According to the Knight Foundation survey, young people____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"tend to voice their opinions in cyberspace."},{"key":"B","text":"verify news by referring to diverse sources."},{"key":"C","text":"have a strong sense of responsibility."},{"key":"D","text":"like to exchange views on \"distributed trust\"."}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"细节题","stemZh":"根据Knight Foundation的调查,年轻人____","choicesZh":{"A":"倾向于在网络空间表达自己的观点","B":"通过参考多种来源来核实新闻","C":"有很强的责任感","D":"喜欢就\"分布式信任\"交流看法"},"analysis":{"type":"题干 According to the Knight Foundation survey, young people____ 问该调查发现年轻人做了什么，是对调查结论的事实提取。","parse":"主干 young people____，空格处是谓语动词短语，要填一个动作或行为描述。无陷阱。","locateEn":"They cross-check sources and prefer news from different perspectives – especially those that are open about any bias.","locateZh":"他们交叉核对来源，并偏好来自不同视角的新闻——尤其是那些坦诚承认自身偏见的来源。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"原文谈的是\"核验\"和\"主动求异\"，从未提及年轻人\"在网上发表意见\"。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"选项 B 同时覆盖了\"verify/核验\"和\"diverse sources/多来源\"两个原文要素，与预判一一对位，是最完整的同义改写。","pairs":[{"orig":"They cross-check sources and prefer news from different perspectives","opt":"verify news by referring to diverse sources"},{"orig":"they use \"distributed trust\" to verify stories","opt":"verify news by referring to diverse sources"}]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"P3 段末引文 Many young people assume a great deal of personal responsibility for educating themselves 确实提到\"责任感\"，但这是段末附加的总结，并非 Knight 调查\"发现\"的核心动作；题干问的是\"调查发现年轻人怎么做\"，C 把附加补充当作核心发现。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"distributed trust 是研究者起的方法名（年轻人\"如何\"验证新闻的机制），不是年轻人之间\"讨论的话题\"。D 把方法名误读成讨论主题。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":29,"stem":"The Barna survey found that a main cause for the fake news problem is____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"readers' outdated values."},{"key":"B","text":"journalists' biased reporting."},{"key":"C","text":"readers' misinterpretation."},{"key":"D","text":"journalists' made-up stories."}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"细节题","stemZh":"Barna调查发现假新闻问题的一个主要原因是____","choicesZh":{"A":"读者过时的价值观","B":"记者有偏见的报道","C":"读者的误读","D":"记者编造的故事"},"analysis":{"type":"题干 The Barna survey found that a main cause for the fake news problem is____ 是对该调查发现的归因结论的事实提取。","parse":"主干 a main cause...is____，is 后接表语，需填\"原因\"性质的名词短语。无陷阱。","locateEn":"A survey by Barna research group found the top reason given by Americans for the fake news phenomenon is \"reader error,\" more so than made-up stories or factual mistakes in reporting. About a third say the problem of fake news lies in \"misinterpretation or exaggeration of actual news\" via social media.","locateZh":"Barna 调查机构的一项调查发现，美国人为\"假新闻现象\"列出的首要原因是\"读者错误\"，其权重高于编造的新闻和报道中的事实错误。约三分之一的人认为，假新闻的问题在于通过社交媒体对真实新闻进行\"误读或夸大\"。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"原文从未提及\"读者价值观过时\"；reader error 指的是\"误读/夸大\"行为，不是价值观问题。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"原文明说 reader error 的权重 more so than made-up stories or factual mistakes in reporting——即\"超过\"了记者层面的错误；B 把次要因素当主要原因，方向反了。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"在 reader error 的所有可能改写中，C 用原词 misinterpretation 与所有格 readers' 复合，最贴近原文表述；与预判完全对位。","pairs":[{"orig":"the top reason given by Americans for the fake news phenomenon is \"reader error,\"","opt":"readers' misinterpretation"},{"orig":"misinterpretation or exaggeration of actual news","opt":"readers' misinterpretation"}]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"原文中 made-up stories（编造的新闻）正是 top reason 用 more so than 否定掉的\"次要因素\"；D 把它当成主要原因，与原文比较关系正相反。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":30,"stem":"Which of the following would be the best title for the text?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"A Rise in Critical Skills for Sharing News Online."},{"key":"B","text":"A Counteraction Against the Over-tweeting Trend."},{"key":"C","text":"The Accumulation of Mutual Trust on Social Media."},{"key":"D","text":"The Platforms for Projection of Personal Interests."}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"主旨题","stemZh":"以下哪项是本文的最佳标题?","choicesZh":{"A":"在线分享新闻的批判性技能的提升","B":"对过度发推特趋势的反制","C":"社交媒体上相互信任的积累","D":"个人兴趣投射的平台"},"analysis":{"type":"题干 best title for the text 求覆盖全文的概括性标题，本质是问主旨。","parse":"Which of the following would be the best title for the text?，which 引导的特殊疑问句，would be 虚拟语气表\"最适合\"，无陷阱。","locateEn":"","locateZh":"","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"选项 A 把全文两大主题（批判性技能 + 在线分享）合在一个标题里，与末段并列结构一一对位，是唯一不以偏概全的概括。","pairs":[{"orig":"they reveal a mental discipline in thinking skills – and in their choices on when to share on social media","opt":"A Rise in Critical Skills for Sharing News Online"},{"orig":"beef up their media literacy skills","opt":"A Rise in Critical Skills for Sharing News Online"}]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"A Counteraction Against the Over-tweeting Trend 只对位 P1 总统过度发推这一引子；全文重心是年轻人的\"分辨/分享\"心智，不是反对发推。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"The Accumulation of Mutual Trust on Social Media 暗示\"信任在累积\"；全文恰恰说 distrust has risen toward all media 与 44 percent of Facebook users rarely or never trust news——是不信任在上升，不是信任在累积。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"The Platforms for Projection of Personal Interests 只对位 P5 中 re-share news as a projection of their values and interests 的局部表述，且把它从论据扭曲为主旨；全文主题不是\"平台供个人投射利益\"，而是\"年轻人的批判性/责任\"。","pairs":[]}}}}}]},{"id":"section2-part-a-3","kind":"reading","title":"Text 3","textNo":3,"directions":"","points":10,"passage":{"title":"Text 3","paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section2-part-a-3-1","text":"Any fair-minded assessment of the dangers of the deal between Britain's National Health Service (NHS) and DeepMind must start by acknowledging that both sides mean well. DeepMind is one of the leading artificial intelligence (AI) companies in the world. The potential of this work applied to healthcare is very great, but it could also lead to further concentration of power in the tech giants. It is against that background that the information commissioner, Elizabeth Denham, has issued her damning verdict against the Royal Free hospital trust under the NHS, which handed over to DeepMind the records of 1.6 million patients in 2015 on the basis of a vague agreement which took far too little account of the patients' rights and their expectations of privacy."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-3-2","text":"DeepMind has almost apologized. The NHS trust has mended its ways. Further arrangements – and there may be many – between the NHS and DeepMind will be carefully scrutinised to ensure that all necessary permissions have been asked of patients and all unnecessary data has been cleaned. There are lessons about informed patient consent to learn. But privacy is not the only angle in this case and not even the most important. Ms Denham chose to concentrate the blame on the NHS trust, since under existing law it \"controlled\" the data and DeepMind merely \"processed\" it. But this distinction misses the point that it is processing and aggregation, not the mere possession of bits, that gives the data value."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-3-3","text":"The great question is who should benefit from the analysis of all the data that our lives now generate. Privacy law builds on the concept of damage to an individual from identifiable knowledge about them. That misses the way the surveillance economy works. The data of an individual there gains its value only when it is compared with the data of countless millions more."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-3-4","text":"The use of privacy law to curb the tech giants in this instance feels slightly maladapted. This practice does not address the real worry. It is not enough to say that the algorithms DeepMind develops will benefit patients and save lives. What matters is that they will belong to a private monopoly which developed them using public resources. If software promises to save lives on the scale that drugs now can, big data may be expected to behave as a big pharma has done. We are still at the beginning of this revolution and small choices now may turn out to have gigantic consequences later. A long struggle will be needed to avoid a future of digital feudalism. Ms Denham's report is a welcome start."}]},"questions":[{"n":31,"stem":"What is true of the agreement between the NHS and DeepMind?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"It caused conflicts among tech giants."},{"key":"B","text":"It failed to pay due attention to patients' rights."},{"key":"C","text":"It fell short of the latter's expectations."},{"key":"D","text":"It put both sides into a dangerous situation."}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"细节题","stemZh":"关于NHS与DeepMind之间的协议,以下哪项是正确的?","choicesZh":{"A":"它在科技巨头之间引发了冲突。","B":"它未能对患者权利给予应有的关注。","C":"它未能达到后者的期望。","D":"它使双方都陷入危险境地。"},"analysis":{"type":"题干 What is true of the agreement between the NHS and DeepMind? 用 \"What is true of\" 直接问关于某具体对象的客观事实，无 infer/imply/suggest 一类推断词，也不问全篇主旨或作者态度。","parse":"What is true of the agreement between the NHS and DeepMind? 是一个标准的 wh-疑问句，主干为 \"What is true of X\"，of 后面挂一个由两个 of 短语层层修饰的名词短语 \"the agreement between the NHS and DeepMind\"——the agreement 是中心词，between... 限定双方主体。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。","locateEn":"It is against that background that the information commissioner, Elizabeth Denham, has issued her damning verdict against the Royal Free hospital trust under the NHS, which handed over to DeepMind the records of 1.6 million patients in 2015 on the basis of a vague agreement which took far too little account of the patients' rights and their expectations of privacy.","locateZh":"正是在这一背景下，信息专员 Elizabeth Denham 对 NHS 旗下的 Royal Free 医院信托基金做出了严厉的裁决——该信托在 2015 年依据一份措辞含糊的协议，把 160 万患者的记录交给了 DeepMind，而这份协议远远没有充分顾及患者的权利及其对隐私的期待。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"It caused conflicts among tech giants. 的\"科技巨头间的冲突\"在 P1 完全没有对应：P1 仅提到 DeepMind 是 leading artificial intelligence (AI) companies，并担忧further concentration of power in the tech giants——是\"权力集中\"，不是\"巨头间冲突\"。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"failed to pay due attention to patients' rights 与定位句的\"远未顾及患者权利\"在语义上完全对应，符合预判，B 即正确项。","pairs":[{"orig":"took far too little account of the patients' rights and their expectations of privacy","opt":"failed to pay due attention to patients' rights"}]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"It fell short of the latter's expectations. 中 the latter（后者）按题干语序指 DeepMind，意思变成\"协议没达到 DeepMind 的期待\"；但 P1 expectations of privacy 的主体是\"the patients\"，不是 DeepMind。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"It put both sides into a dangerous situation. 把协议描绘成让 NHS 与 DeepMind 双双陷入危险；P1 只说the dangers of the deal是从公众/患者角度出发要审视的风险，并未说协议把 NHS 和 DeepMind 双方推入险地。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":32,"stem":"The NHS trust responded to Denham's verdict with____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"empty promises."},{"key":"B","text":"tough resistance."},{"key":"C","text":"necessary adjustments."},{"key":"D","text":"sincere apologies."}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"细节题","stemZh":"NHS信托机构对Denham的裁决作出回应时采取了____","choicesZh":{"A":"空洞的承诺。","B":"强硬的抵制。","C":"必要的调整。","D":"真诚的道歉。"},"analysis":{"type":"题干 The NHS trust responded to Denham's verdict with____ 是一个待补全陈述句，问\"以何种方式回应\"，要的是文中描述的具体动作，非推断、非主旨、非态度。","parse":"The NHS trust responded to Denham's verdict with____ 是 SVOO 结构的待补全陈述：主语 The NHS trust，谓语 responded to，宾语 Denham's verdict，with____ 引导方式状语等待选项延续。结构清晰，无陷阱。","locateEn":"DeepMind has almost apologized. The NHS trust has mended its ways.","locateZh":"DeepMind 已经近乎道歉，NHS 信托基金则已经改正了自己的做法。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"empty promises（空头承诺）暗含\"嘴上说做、实际未做\"的负面评价；但 has mended its ways 用现在完成时强调动作已完成，且原文并未指责 NHS trust 言行不一。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"tough resistance（强硬抵抗）意味着对裁决不服并对抗；但定位句明说 NHS trust 已 mended its ways，是接受并整改，方向相反。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"necessary adjustments 与 has mended its ways 在动作语义上完全等价，符合预判；其余三项均偏离\"已做整改\"的中性事实，C 即正确项。","pairs":[{"orig":"The NHS trust has mended its ways","opt":"necessary adjustments"}]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"sincere apologies（真诚道歉）对应 P2 第一句 DeepMind has almost apologized，但道歉的主体是 DeepMind 而非 NHS trust；况且 almost apologized（几乎道歉）也与 sincere（真诚的）有偏差。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":33,"stem":"The author argues in Paragraph 2 that____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"privacy protection must be secured at all costs."},{"key":"B","text":"leaking patients' data is worse than selling it."},{"key":"C","text":"making profits from patients' data is illegal."},{"key":"D","text":"the value of data comes from the processing of it."}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"主旨题","stemZh":"作者在第2段中论证____","choicesZh":{"A":"隐私保护必须不惜一切代价得到保障。","B":"泄露患者数据比出售患者数据更糟糕。","C":"从患者数据中获利是非法的。","D":"数据的价值来自对数据的处理。"},"analysis":{"type":"The author argues in Paragraph 2 that____ 问的是某一段中作者主张的核心论点，不是某一处具体细节，需要把第二段整体读完后归纳。","parse":"The author argues in Paragraph 2 that____ 主干 The author argues that____，that 引导宾语从句的内容由选项补全；in Paragraph 2 是地点状语，限定取证范围。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。","locateEn":"","locateZh":"","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"privacy protection must be secured at all costs. 是绝对化命令式断言；P2 却说 privacy is not the only angle in this case and not even the most important——作者明确淡化隐私的中心性，与 A 项方向相反。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"leaking patients' data is worse than selling it. 制造一个「泄露 vs 出售」的比较；P2 通篇没有出现这两种行为的比较，更没有判定孰更糟。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"making profits from patients' data is illegal. 把「利用患者数据牟利」判定为违法；P2 只说数据价值生于处理，并讨论谁该受益，从未做合法/非法的法律判断。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"the value of data comes from the processing of it 把作者主张句的核心语义「价值源于处理」忠实复现，符合预判，其余三项要么超出原文、要么方向不对，D 即正确项。","pairs":[{"orig":"it is processing and aggregation, not the mere possession of bits, that gives the data value","opt":"the value of data comes from the processing of it"}]}}}}},{"n":34,"stem":"According to the last paragraph, the real worry arising from this deal is____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"the vicious rivalry among big pharmas."},{"key":"B","text":"the ineffective enforcement of privacy law."},{"key":"C","text":"the uncontrolled use of new software."},{"key":"D","text":"the monopoly of big data by tech giants."}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"细节题","stemZh":"根据最后一段,这笔交易引发的真正担忧是____","choicesZh":{"A":"大型制药公司之间的恶性竞争。","B":"隐私法的执行不力。","C":"新软件的不受控制使用。","D":"科技巨头对大数据的垄断。"},"analysis":{"type":"题干 According to the last paragraph, the real worry arising from this deal is____ 用 According to + 段位 直接问 \"the real worry\" 是什么——属于段内细节抽取，不需跨段综合。","parse":"According to the last paragraph, the real worry arising from this deal is____ 主干为 the real worry is____；arising from this deal 是现在分词短语作 worry 的后置定语，限定\"由这桩交易引起\"。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。","locateEn":"This practice does not address the real worry. It is not enough to say that the algorithms DeepMind develops will benefit patients and save lives. What matters is that they will belong to a private monopoly which developed them using public resources.","locateZh":"这种做法（仅用隐私法约束）并没有应对真正的担忧。仅仅说 DeepMind 开发的算法将造福患者、挽救生命还不够；真正要紧的是，这些算法将归属于一家用公共资源开发它们的私人垄断企业。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"the vicious rivalry among big pharmas. 描述\"大药企之间的恶性竞争\"；P4 提到 big pharma 仅是用作类比（big data may be expected to behave as a big pharma has done），并未描述大药企间的恶性竞争。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"the ineffective enforcement of privacy law. 谈隐私法执行不力；P4 说The use of privacy law to curb the tech giants in this instance feels slightly maladapted——隐私法本身不对症，而非\"执行不力\"；且作者明说这\"不是真担忧\"。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"the uncontrolled use of new software. 把担忧定为新软件被无序使用；P4 并非担心软件\"被滥用\"，而是担心算法\"归私人垄断\"，焦点在所有权而非使用秩序。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"the monopoly of big data by tech giants 与 they will belong to a private monopoly which developed them using public resources 在核心语义\"私人垄断算法/数据\"上完全等值，符合预判，D 即正确项。","pairs":[{"orig":"they will belong to a private monopoly which developed them using public resources","opt":"the monopoly of big data by tech giants"}]}}}}},{"n":35,"stem":"The author's attitude toward the application of AI to healthcare is____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"ambiguous."},{"key":"B","text":"cautious."},{"key":"C","text":"appreciative."},{"key":"D","text":"contemptuous."}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"态度题","stemZh":"作者对人工智能在医疗保健领域应用的态度是____","choicesZh":{"A":"模棱两可的。","B":"谨慎的。","C":"赞赏的。","D":"轻蔑的。"},"analysis":{"type":"The author's attitude toward the application of AI to healthcare is____ 显式问态度（attitude），选项也是清一色态度形容词（ambiguous/cautious/appreciative/contemptuous），属典型态度题。","parse":"The author's attitude toward the application of AI to healthcare is____ 主干 The author's attitude is____；toward... 是介词短语作 attitude 的后置定语，限定态度指向的对象。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。","locateEn":"","locateZh":"","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"ambiguous. 意为\"态度不明、模棱两可\"；但全文从 damning verdict 到 digital feudalism 都给出了明确的警示性立场，并非\"看不出倾向\"。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"cautious. 恰好对应\"承认 AI 价值（让步）+ 反复警示风险（转折）\"的极性结构，是四个态度形容词中唯一能覆盖全文双面性的选项，符合预判。","pairs":[{"orig":"Any fair-minded assessment of the dangers of the deal between Britain's National Health Service (NHS) and DeepMind must start by acknowledging that both sides mean well.","opt":"cautious."},{"orig":"small choices now may turn out to have gigantic consequences later. A long struggle will be needed to avoid a future of digital feudalism.","opt":"cautious."}]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"appreciative. 意为\"赞赏\"；P1 虽肯定 DeepMind 是 one of the leading artificial intelligence (AI) companies in the world，但全文重心在警示风险，最末段更呼吁 A long struggle，态度远非赞赏。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"contemptuous. 意为\"鄙视、不屑\"；作者明说 both sides mean well、Ms Denham's report is a welcome start，从未表达对 AI 应用于医疗的不屑或鄙视。","pairs":[]}}}}}]},{"id":"section2-part-a-4","kind":"reading","title":"Text 4","textNo":4,"directions":"","points":10,"passage":{"title":"Text 4","paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section2-part-a-4-1","text":"The U.S. Postal Service (USPS) continues to bleed red ink. It reported a net loss of $5.6 billion for fiscal 2016, the 10th straight year its expenses have exceeded revenue. Meanwhile, it has more than $120 billion in unfunded liabilities, mostly for employee health and retirement costs. There are many reasons this formerly stable federal institution finds itself at the brink of bankruptcy. Fundamentally, the USPS is in a historic squeeze between technological change that has permanently decreased demand for its bread-and-butter product, first-class mail, and a regulatory structure that denies management the flexibility to adjust its operations to the new reality."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-4-2","text":"And interest groups ranging from postal unions to greeting-card makers exert self-interested pressure on the USPS's ultimate overseer – Congress – insisting that whatever else happens to the Postal Service, aspects of the status quo they depend on get protected. This is why repeated attempts at reform legislation have failed in recent years, leaving the Postal Service unable to pay its bills except by deferring vital modernization."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-4-3","text":"Now comes word that everyone involved – Democrats, Republicans, the Postal Service, the unions and the system's heaviest users – has finally agreed on a plan to fix the system. Legislation is moving through the House that would save USPS an estimated $28.6 billion over five years, which could help pay for new vehicles, among other survival measures. Most of the money would come from a penny-per-letter permanent rate increase and from shifting postal retirees into Medicare. The latter step would largely offset the financial burden of annually pre-funding retiree health care, thus addressing a long-standing complaint by the USPS and its unions."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-4-4","text":"If it clears the House, this measure would still have to get through the Senate – where someone is bound to point out that it amounts to the bare, bare minimum necessary to keep the Postal Service afloat, not comprehensive reform. There's no change to collective bargaining at the USPS, a major omission considering that personnel accounts for 80 percent of the agency's costs. Also missing is any discussion of eliminating Saturday letter delivery. That common-sense change enjoys wide public support and would save the USPS $2 billion per year. But postal special-interest groups seem to have killed it, at least in the House. The emerging consensus around the bill is a sign that legislators are getting frightened about a politically embarrassing short-term collapse at the USPS. It is not, however, a sign that they're getting serious about transforming the postal system for the 21st century."}]},"questions":[{"n":36,"stem":"The financial problem with the USPS is caused partly by____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"its unbalanced budget."},{"key":"B","text":"its rigid management."},{"key":"C","text":"the cost for technical upgrading."},{"key":"D","text":"the withdrawal of bank support."}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"细节题","stemZh":"美国邮政署的财政问题部分是由什么造成的","choicesZh":{"A":"其不平衡的预算。","B":"其僵化的管理。","C":"技术升级的成本。","D":"银行支持的撤回。"},"analysis":{"type":"The financial problem with the USPS is caused partly by 是一个待补全的陈述句，问的是导致财务问题的部分原因，属于对具体事实的提问。","parse":"The financial problem with the USPS is caused partly by____ 是一个待补全的被动句，主语 financial problem，谓语 is caused，partly 是状语限定“部分原因”，选项接在 by 后做行为者。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。","locateEn":"Fundamentally, the USPS is in a historic squeeze between technological change that has permanently decreased demand for its bread-and-butter product, first-class mail, and a regulatory structure that denies management the flexibility to adjust its operations to the new reality.","locateZh":"从根本上说，USPS 正陷于一场历史性的双重挤压：一面是技术变革永久压低了其核心业务——一类邮件——的需求，一面是监管体制不让管理层有足够灵活性去根据新现实调整运营。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"its unbalanced budget “预算不平衡”是亏损的表现（expenses have exceeded revenue）而不是原因，把“现象”当成“原因”是张冠李戴。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"预判要的是“管理层没有灵活性”，its rigid management 用 rigid 一个词精准捕捉“被剥夺灵活性”的语义，且原文 partly 与选项 partly 完美对应——本题为部分原因，不必把所有成因都囊括。","pairs":[{"orig":"a regulatory structure that denies management the flexibility to adjust its operations","opt":"its rigid management"}]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"原文谈的是 technological change that has permanently decreased demand——技术变革压低需求，而非 the cost for technical upgrading “技术升级的成本”；原文从未提及升级技术的开销。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"原文从未提及 the withdrawal of bank support “银行撤回支持”——USPS 是联邦机构，融资和银行无关，本项完全是原文之外的概念。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":37,"stem":"According to Paragraph 2, the USPS fails to modernize itself due to____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"the interference from interest groups."},{"key":"B","text":"the inadequate funding from Congress."},{"key":"C","text":"the shrinking demand for postal service."},{"key":"D","text":"the incompetence of postal unions."}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"细节题","stemZh":"根据第二段,美国邮政署未能实现自身现代化是由于","choicesZh":{"A":"利益集团的干预。","B":"国会提供的资金不足。","C":"邮政服务需求的萎缩。","D":"邮政工会的无能。"},"analysis":{"type":"fails to modernize itself due to 用 due to 直接问原因，属于段内事实题。","parse":"the USPS fails to modernize itself due to____ 中 fails to do 表示“未能做到”，itself 反身指代 USPS，due to 引出原因。注意 fail 本身含轻微否定色彩，但本题问的仍是“原因”，不要被否定迷惑。","locateEn":"And interest groups ranging from postal unions to greeting-card makers exert self-interested pressure on the USPS's ultimate overseer – Congress – insisting that whatever else happens to the Postal Service, aspects of the status quo they depend on get protected. This is why repeated attempts at reform legislation have failed in recent years, leaving the Postal Service unable to pay its bills except by deferring vital modernization.","locateZh":"从邮政工会到贺卡商，各种利益集团向 USPS 的最终监管者——国会——施加利己的压力，坚持要求无论邮政服务发生什么变化，他们所依赖的现状的某些方面都要被保护。这就是近年来改革立法屡次失败、USPS 只能靠推迟关键的现代化来勉强支付账单的原因。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"预判要的是“利益集团对国会的施压”，the interference from interest groups 用 interference 概括“施压 + 要求保护现状”的整体行为，正是定位句因果链的“因”端。","pairs":[{"orig":"interest groups ranging from postal unions to greeting-card makers exert self-interested pressure","opt":"the interference from interest groups"}]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"原文谈的是国会受利益集团施压、改革立法失败，从未说 the inadequate funding from Congress “国会拨款不足”；事实上第 3 段还提到国会正在推进的法案能省下 286 亿美元。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"the shrinking demand for postal service “邮政业务需求萎缩”是 permanently decreased demand 的转述，属第 1 段内容，而本题已指定 According to Paragraph 2，超出段落范围；同时“需求萎缩”是财务问题的原因，不是不能现代化的原因，是答非所问。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"原文将 postal unions 列为施压一方而非被动方，且并未评价其能力。the incompetence of postal unions “邮政工会的无能”与原文中“工会主动施压”的形象相反，属与原文相反。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":38,"stem":"The long-standing complaint by the USPS and its unions can be addressed by____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"removing its burden of retiree health care."},{"key":"B","text":"making more investment in new vehicles."},{"key":"C","text":"adopting a new rate-increase mechanism."},{"key":"D","text":"attracting more first-class mail users."}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"细节题","stemZh":"美国邮政署及其工会长期以来的抱怨可以通过什么来解决","choicesZh":{"A":"消除其退休人员医疗保健的负担。","B":"对新车辆进行更多投资。","C":"采用新的费率上涨机制。","D":"吸引更多的一类邮件用户。"},"analysis":{"type":"can be addressed by 问的是“通过什么手段可以解决”，属于对原文中明确解决方案的转述。","parse":"The long-standing complaint by the USPS and its unions can be addressed by____ 是被动陈述句。by 在主语后表“由谁提出的抱怨”，by 在 addressed 后表“通过何种方式解决”——同一句出现两个 by，注意区分指向。","locateEn":"Most of the money would come from a penny-per-letter permanent rate increase and from shifting postal retirees into Medicare. The latter step would largely offset the financial burden of annually pre-funding retiree health care, thus addressing a long-standing complaint by the USPS and its unions.","locateZh":"这笔钱大部分来自每封信永久涨价一便士，以及把邮政退休员工转入 Medicare。后一项措施将在很大程度上抵消每年预提退休人员医保的财务负担，从而解决 USPS 及其工会长期以来的抱怨。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"预判要的是“免除预提医保负担”，removing its burden of retiree health care 用 removing burden 精准捕捉 offset financial burden 的核心动作，是最贴合定位句的同义改写。","pairs":[{"orig":"offset the financial burden of annually pre-funding retiree health care","opt":"removing its burden of retiree health care"}]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"making more investment in new vehicles “加大新车投资”在原文 which could help pay for new vehicles 出现，但那是省下的钱可能的用途之一，不是用于“解决长期抱怨”的手段；属张冠李戴——把“省下钱后的用途”嫁接到“解决抱怨的手段”上。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"原文 a penny-per-letter permanent rate increase 是涨价措施，但这是“钱从哪来”的资金来源，不是“解决长期抱怨”的手段；且 a new rate-increase mechanism “新的涨价机制”也把“一次性涨价一便士”偷换成“机制”，是双重偷换概念。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"原文从未提及 attracting more first-class mail users “吸引更多一类邮件用户”；第 1 段虽提到 first-class mail 需求下降，但第 3 段的解决方案中并无任何“开发用户”措施。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":39,"stem":"In the last paragraph, the author seems to view legislators with","options":[{"key":"A","text":"respect."},{"key":"B","text":"tolerance."},{"key":"C","text":"discontent."},{"key":"D","text":"gratitude."}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"态度题","stemZh":"在最后一段中,作者似乎以什么态度看待立法者","choicesZh":{"A":"尊重。","B":"容忍。","C":"不满。","D":"感激。"},"analysis":{"type":"the author seems to view legislators with 用 view ... with 直接问作者持何种态度，是典型态度题句式。","parse":"In the last paragraph, the author seems to view legislators with 中 seems to 是缓和语气，view sb with sth 表示“以某种态度看待某人”，with 后接情感名词。结构清晰，无陷阱。","locateEn":"","locateZh":"","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"respect “尊重”是正面态度，与原文 not ... getting serious 的负面评价极性相反。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"tolerance “容忍”暗示“虽不赞同但能接受”，程度偏温和；而原文连用 bare, bare minimum（“最最低限度”，bare 重复加强）、not ... getting serious、a major omission 等强烈否定，远超容忍的温度——属程度词偷改。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"预判要的是“不满/失望”，discontent 是态度词中最贴合“否定改革诚意 + 暗讽动机不纯”双重负面判断的选项。","pairs":[{"orig":"It is not, however, a sign that they're getting serious about transforming the postal system for the 21st century","opt":"discontent"},{"orig":"legislators are getting frightened about a politically embarrassing short-term collapse","opt":"discontent"}]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"gratitude “感激”是正面态度，与作者对议员“动机不纯、缺乏诚意”的批评完全冲突。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":40,"stem":"Which of the following would be the best title for the text?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"The USPS Starts to Miss Its Good Old Days."},{"key":"B","text":"The Postal Service: Keep Away from My Cheese."},{"key":"C","text":"The USPS: Chronic Illness Requires a Quick Cure."},{"key":"D","text":"The Postal Service Needs More Than a Band-Aid."}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"主旨题","stemZh":"以下哪项是本文的最佳标题","choicesZh":{"A":"美国邮政署开始怀念它的美好旧时光。","B":"邮政服务:别动我的奶酪。","C":"美国邮政署:慢性病需要快速治愈。","D":"邮政服务需要的不仅仅是创可贴。"},"analysis":{"type":"Which of the following would be the best title for the text 问的是全文标题，属于典型主旨题。","parse":"Which of the following would be the best title for the text 是 wh- 疑问句，would be 是虚拟语气表“最适合做”。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。","locateEn":"","locateZh":"","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"The USPS Starts to Miss Its Good Old Days “USPS 开始怀念过去的好日子”暗示一种怀旧、感伤的基调，而原文是冷峻分析当前困境与对策不足——情感色彩完全不符，且文中无任何怀旧描写。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"The Postal Service: Keep Away from My Cheese “邮政服务：别动我的奶酪”虽呼应了 P2 利益集团保护现状的描写，但只覆盖一个局部段落，未触及“病重”与“对策不足”两大主线——以偏概全。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"The USPS: Chronic Illness Requires a Quick Cure 前半“慢性病”确与原文吻合，但后半“需要速效药”与原文 not comprehensive reform 所主张的“需全面、深度改革”恰好相反——原文嫌当前法案“太速成、不彻底”，而本选项却主张“快治”。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"预判要的是“病重 + 创可贴不够”，The Postal Service Needs More Than a Band-Aid 用 Band-Aid 隐喻精准捕捉 bare minimum 的语义，并用 more than 直接表达“远远不够”——两端都覆盖。","pairs":[{"orig":"it amounts to the bare, bare minimum necessary to keep the Postal Service afloat, not comprehensive reform","opt":"The Postal Service Needs More Than a Band-Aid"},{"orig":"the brink of bankruptcy","opt":"The Postal Service Needs More Than a Band-Aid"}]}}}}}]},{"id":"section2-part-b","kind":"ordering","title":"Section II Part B 段落排序","directions":"Directions: The following paragraphs are given in a wrong order. For questions 41 -45, you are required to reorganize these paragraphs into a coherent text by choosing from the list A-G and filling them into the numbered boxes. Paragraphs C and F have been correctly placed. Mark your answers on the ANSWER SHEET. (10 points)","points":10,"paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section2-part-b-1","label":"A","text":"In December of 1869, Congress appointed a commission to select a site and prepare plans and cost estimates for a new State Department Building. The commission was also to consider possible arrangements for the War and Navy Departments. To the horror of some who expected a Greek Revival twin of the Treasury Building to be erected on the other side of the White House, the elaborate French Second Empire style design by Alfred Mullett was selected, and construction of a building to house all three departments began in June of 1871."},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-2","label":"B","text":"Completed in 1875, the State Department's south wing was the first to be occupied, with its elegant four-story library (completed in 1876), Diplomatic Reception Room, and Secretary's office decorated with carved wood, Oriental rugs, and stenciled wall patterns. The Navy Department moved into the east wing in 1879, where elaborate wall and ceiling stenciling and marquetry floors decorated the office of the Secretary."},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-3","label":"C","text":"The State, War, and Navy Building, as it was originally known, housed the three Executive Branch Departments most intimately associated with formulating and conducting the nation's foreign policy in the last quarter of the nineteenth century and the first quarter of the twentieth century – the period when the United States emerged as an international power. The building has housed some of the nation's most significant diplomats and politicians and has been the scene of many historic events."},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-4","label":"D","text":"Many of the most celebrated national figures have participated in historical events that have taken place within the EEOB's granite walls. Theodore and Franklin D. Roosevelt, William Howard Taft, Dwight D. Eisenhower, Lyndon B. Johnson, Gerald Ford, and George H. W. Bush all had offices in this building before becoming president. It has housed 16 Secretaries of the Navy, 21 Secretaries of War, and 24 Secretaries of State. Winston Churchill once walked its corridors and Japanese emissaries met here with Secretary of State Cordell Hull after the bombing of Pearl Harbor."},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-5","label":"E","text":"The Eisenhower Executive Office Building (EEOB) commands a unique position in both the national history and the architectural heritage of the United States. Designed by Supervising Architect of the Treasury, Alfred B. Mullett, it was built from 1871 to 1888 to house the growing staffs of the State, War, and Navy Departments, and is considered one of the best examples of French Second Empire architecture in the country."},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-6","label":"F","text":"Construction took 17 years as the building slowly rose wing by wing. When the EEOB was finished, it was the largest office building in Washington, with nearly 2 miles of black and white tiled corridors. Almost all of the interior detail is of cast iron or plaster; the use of wood was minimized to insure fire safety. Eight monumental curving staircases of granite with over 4,000 individually cast bronze balusters are capped by four skylight domes and two stained glass rotundas."},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-7","label":"G","text":"The history of the EEOB began long before its foundations were laid. The first executive offices were constructed between 1799 and 1820. A series of fires (including those set by the British in 1814) and overcrowded conditions led to the construction of the existing Treasury Building. In 1866, the construction of the North Wing of the Treasury Building necessitated the demolition of the State Department building."}],"choices":[{"key":"A","text":"In December of 1869, Congress appointed a commission to select a site and prepare plans and cost estimates for a new State Department Building. The commission was also to consider possible arrangements for the War and Navy Departments. To the horror of some who expected a Greek Revival twin of the Treasury Building to be erected on the other side of the White House, the elaborate French Second Empire style design by Alfred Mullett was selected, and construction of a building to house all three departments began in June of 1871."},{"key":"B","text":"Completed in 1875, the State Department's south wing was the first to be occupied, with its elegant four-story library (completed in 1876), Diplomatic Reception Room, and Secretary's office decorated with carved wood, Oriental rugs, and stenciled wall patterns. The Navy Department moved into the east wing in 1879, where elaborate wall and ceiling stenciling and marquetry floors decorated the office of the Secretary."},{"key":"C","text":"The State, War, and Navy Building, as it was originally known, housed the three Executive Branch Departments most intimately associated with formulating and conducting the nation's foreign policy in the last quarter of the nineteenth century and the first quarter of the twentieth century – the period when the United States emerged as an international power. The building has housed some of the nation's most significant diplomats and politicians and has been the scene of many historic events."},{"key":"D","text":"Many of the most celebrated national figures have participated in historical events that have taken place within the EEOB's granite walls. Theodore and Franklin D. Roosevelt, William Howard Taft, Dwight D. Eisenhower, Lyndon B. Johnson, Gerald Ford, and George H. W. Bush all had offices in this building before becoming president. It has housed 16 Secretaries of the Navy, 21 Secretaries of War, and 24 Secretaries of State. Winston Churchill once walked its corridors and Japanese emissaries met here with Secretary of State Cordell Hull after the bombing of Pearl Harbor."},{"key":"E","text":"The Eisenhower Executive Office Building (EEOB) commands a unique position in both the national history and the architectural heritage of the United States. Designed by Supervising Architect of the Treasury, Alfred B. Mullett, it was built from 1871 to 1888 to house the growing staffs of the State, War, and Navy Departments, and is considered one of the best examples of French Second Empire architecture in the country."},{"key":"F","text":"Construction took 17 years as the building slowly rose wing by wing. When the EEOB was finished, it was the largest office building in Washington, with nearly 2 miles of black and white tiled corridors. Almost all of the interior detail is of cast iron or plaster; the use of wood was minimized to insure fire safety. Eight monumental curving staircases of granite with over 4,000 individually cast bronze balusters are capped by four skylight domes and two stained glass rotundas."},{"key":"G","text":"The history of the EEOB began long before its foundations were laid. The first executive offices were constructed between 1799 and 1820. A series of fires (including those set by the British in 1814) and overcrowded conditions led to the construction of the existing Treasury Building. In 1866, the construction of the North Wing of the Treasury Building necessitated the demolition of the State Department building."}],"questions":[{"n":41,"stem":"","options":[],"answer":"E"},{"n":42,"stem":"","options":[],"answer":"G"},{"n":43,"stem":"","options":[],"answer":"A"},{"n":44,"stem":"","options":[],"answer":"B"},{"n":45,"stem":"","options":[],"answer":"D"}],"order":["E","C","G","A","F","B","D"],"fixed":{"1":"C","4":"F"},"chain":"41. → C → 42. → 43. → F → 44. → 45.","orderChain":"正确顺序 41 E → 预给 C → 42 G → 43 A → 预给 F → 44 B → 45 D","seams":["【引出后一段】 段 C 词汇复现：E 段尾点出 State/War/Navy 三部门，C 段首用「原名 State, War, and Navy Building」复现并承接 。E 收尾抛出三个部门名 State, War, and Navy Departments，紧接的给定段 C 用 as it was originally known（最初被称为）把这三部门拼成这栋楼的原名，词汇复现 State/War/Navy 把两段钉在一起，41 位正放在首段 E。","【承接前一段】 段 C 指代衔接：C 段尾抛出「历史事件」总话头，G 段首 The history of the EEOB 顺势展开这栋楼的历史源头 。给定段 C 段尾 has been the scene of many historic events 把话头引向「历史」，紧接的 42 位用 G：段首 The history of the EEOB began long before its foundations were laid 中 the EEOB 回指 C/E 已确立的这栋楼，并从「历史」一词接力展开它的由来，承接成立。","【引出后一段】 段 A 语义承接：G 段尾停在 1866 年拆旧楼，A 段首接 1869 年立项，时间线顺承 。G 结尾 In 1866 ... the demolition of the State Department building 与 A 开头 In December of 1869, Congress appointed a commission 是同一条时间线（1866→1869）的相邻两步：拆了旧楼，三年后国会就设委员会建新楼，年份递进、事件接续。","【承接前一段】 段 G 语义承接：A 段首 1869 年立项紧接 G 段尾 1866 年拆楼，年份递进 。43 位刚定的前邻是 42 位的 G，其段尾停在 In 1866 ... the demolition of the State Department building；A 段首 In December of 1869, Congress appointed a commission 把时间推进到 1869 年立项，1866→1869 同一条沿革线顺承。","【引出后一段】 段 F 语义承接：A 段尾 1871 年开工，给定段 F 段首回答「建了 17 年」，开工→工期顺承 。A 收尾 construction ... began in June of 1871（1871 年 6 月开工）正好被给定段 F 的段首 Construction took 17 years 承接：先开工、再讲工期，动作链 began→took 17 years 紧扣，43 位放 A 后与给定 F 严丝合缝。","【承接前一段】 段 F 语义承接：F 讲整楼逐翼建造，B 段首 Completed in 1875 接「建成入驻」，在建→建成顺承 。给定段 F 讲 the building slowly rose wing by wing（一翼一翼地建）；44 位的 B 段首 Completed in 1875, the State Department's south wing was the first to be occupied 顺势讲哪一翼先建成、先入驻，从「在建」推进到「建成入驻」，且都扣 wing（翼）这一线索。","【承接前一段】 段 B 语义承接：B 讲各部门入驻办公，D 顺势讲「楼里出过哪些名人」，由建筑转向人物收尾 。B 段尾把各翼入驻、办公室装饰讲完（decorated the office of the Secretary）；45 位的 D 段首 Many of the most celebrated national figures have participated in historical events ... within the EEOB's granite walls 由「楼/办公室」自然转向「楼里出过的人」，是建造-入驻线索讲完后的收尾段。"]},{"id":"section2-part-c","kind":"translation","title":"Section II Part C 英译汉","directions":"Directions: Read the following text carefully and then translate the underlined segments into Chinese. Write your answers on the ANSWER SHEET. ( 10 points)","points":10,"passage":{"paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section2-part-c-1","text":"Shakespeare's lifetime was coincident with a period of extraordinary activity and achievement in the drama. (46) By the date of his birth Europe was witnessing the passing of the religious drama, and the creation of new forms under the incentive of classical tragedy and comedy. These new forms were at first mainly written by scholars and performed by amateurs, but in England, as everywhere else in western Europe, the growth of a class of professional actors was threatening to make the drama popular, whether it should be new or old, classical or medieval, literary or farcical. Court, school, organizations of amateurs, and the traveling actors were all rivals in supplying a widespread desire for dramatic entertainment; and (47) no boy who went to a grammar school could be ignorant that the drama was a form of literature which gave glory to Greece and Rome and might yet bring honor to England."},{"id":"p-section2-part-c-2","text":"When Shakespeare was twelve years old the first public playhouse was built in London. For a time literature showed no interest in this public stage. Plays aiming at literary distinction were written for schools or court, or for the choir boys of St. Paul's and the royal chapel, who, however, gave plays in public as well as at court. (48) But the professional companies prospered in their permanent theaters, and university men with literary ambitions were quick to turn to these theaters as offering a means of livelihood. By the time that Shakespeare was twenty-five, Lyly, Peele, and Greene had made comedies that were at once popular and literary; Kyd had written a tragedy that crowded the pit; and Marlowe had brought poetry and genius to triumph on the common stage – where they had played no part since the death of Euripides. (49) A native literary drama had been created, its alliance with the public playhouses established, and at least some of its great traditions had been begun."},{"id":"p-section2-part-c-3","text":"The development of the Elizabethan drama for the next twenty-five years is of exceptional interest to students of literary history, for in this brief period we may trace the beginning, growth, blossoming, and decay of many kinds of plays, and of many great careers. We are amazed today at the mere number of plays produced, as well as by the number of dramatists writing at the same time for this London of two hundred thousand inhabitants. (50) To realize how great was the dramatic activity, we must remember further that hosts of plays have been lost, and that probably there is no author of note whose entire work has survived."}]},"sentences":[{"n":46,"en":"By the date of his birth Europe was witnessing the passing of the religious drama, and the creation of new forms under the incentive of classical tragedy and comedy.","refZh":"到他（莎士比亚）出生的时候，欧洲正见证着宗教剧的消逝，以及在古典悲剧和喜剧的推动下新戏剧形式的诞生。","analysis":"","chunks":[{"en":"By the date of his birth","lit":"到他出生的时候","role":"（时间状语）","note":"难点： his 回指莎士比亚 + by the date of 译『到……时』 his 是孤立看不出的代词，须按第一步还原表回指 Shakespeare's lifetime，即莎士比亚，可译『他（莎士比亚）出生时』。by the date of 不是『在……的日期旁』，date 此处指时间点，整短语意为『到……这个时候』。"},{"en":"Europe was witnessing the passing of the religious drama,","lit":"欧洲正目睹着宗教剧的消逝","role":"（主干主谓宾（第一个宾语））","note":"难点： witness 熟词僻义『见证/经历』+ passing 僻义『消逝』 witness 此处不是『目击（罪案）』，而是『（某地、某时代）见证、经历』；the passing 不是『通过』，passing 在此是『消逝、消亡、退场』之义（指 the religious drama 宗教剧逐渐退出历史舞台）。这两处熟词僻义是本块难点。"},{"en":"and the creation of new forms","lit":"以及新形式的诞生","role":"（主干宾语（第二个宾语，与 the passing 并列））","note":"难点： creation 与前面 passing 并列、同作 witnessing 的宾语 the creation of new forms 和前面 the passing of the religious drama 由 and 并列，共同作 was witnessing 的宾语：欧洲既目睹旧剧『消逝』、又目睹新形式『诞生』。抽象名词 the creation 可具体化为动作『（新形式的）产生、出现』，避免『新形式的创造』这种生硬译法。"},{"en":"under the incentive of classical tragedy and comedy.","lit":"在古典悲剧和喜剧的激励之下","role":"（介词短语作状语（修饰 creation））","note":"难点： incentive 译『推动/激励』+ classical 译『古典（希腊罗马）的』 incentive 此处是『刺激、推动、激励』，under the incentive of 意为『在……的推动下』；classical 在文学史语境里特指『古典的』，即古希腊罗马的悲剧喜剧（呼应下段 Greece and Rome），不要译成『经典的』。"}]},{"n":47,"en":"no boy who went to a grammar school could be ignorant that the drama was a form of literature which gave glory to Greece and Rome and might yet bring honor to England.","refZh":"凡是上过文法学校的男孩，没有谁会不知道：戏剧是一种文学形式，它曾为希腊和罗马带来荣耀，或许将来还会为英国增光。","analysis":"","chunks":[{"en":"no boy who went to a grammar school could be ignorant","lit":"没有哪个上过文法学校的男孩会不知道","role":"（主干（含 who 定语从句）+ 双重否定）","note":"难点： no…ignorant 双重否定 + grammar school 固定译名 no（否定主语）+ be ignorant（『不知道、无知』本身含否定）= 双重否定，须译成肯定语气：『凡是上过……的男孩，都知道』，或保留『没有谁会不知道』。这是 A6 含蓄否定的盲区，直译『没有男孩能无知』会读不通。grammar school 是历史专名『文法学校』（教授拉丁语法的中学），不要望文译成『语法学校』。"},{"en":"that the drama was a form of literature","lit":"戏剧是文学的一种形式","role":"（ignorant 后的 that 宾语从句（主干））","note":"难点： a form of literature 译『一种文学形式』 that 引导 be ignorant of 的内容从句（of 省略），是『（不）知道』的具体内容；a form of literature 即『一种文学形式／文学样式』，drama 仍按全篇统一译『戏剧』。"},{"en":"which gave glory to Greece and Rome","lit":"这种文学形式曾为希腊和罗马带来荣耀","role":"（which 后置定语从句（修饰 literature））","note":"难点： which 指 a form of literature（即戏剧）+ gave glory to 译『给……带来荣耀』 which 的先行词是 a form of literature（亦即 the drama）；gave glory to 不是『给希腊罗马以荣耀地』，glory 作宾语，意为『（曾）为希腊和罗马增光／带来荣耀』，呼应上文 classical 古典戏剧的辉煌。"},{"en":"and might yet bring honor to England.","lit":"并且或许还会给英国带来荣誉","role":"（与 gave 并列的第二个谓语（同属 which 从句））","note":"难点： yet 译『（将来）还／仍』+ 与 gave glory 并列 and 连接 which 从句内两个谓语 gave… 和 might yet bring…；yet 在此不是『但是』，而是『（迄今尚未、但将来）还会、仍会』之义，might yet bring honor 译『（将来）或许还会给英国带来荣誉』，与前面给希腊罗马带来荣耀形成『过去—未来』呼应。"}]},{"n":48,"en":"But the professional companies prospered in their permanent theaters, and university men with literary ambitions were quick to turn to these theaters as offering a means of livelihood.","refZh":"但是，职业剧团在自己的常设剧场里日渐兴盛，怀有文学抱负的大学才子们也纷纷转向这些剧场，因为剧场为他们提供了一种谋生的手段。","analysis":"","chunks":[{"en":"But the professional companies prospered in their permanent theaters,","lit":"但是职业剧团在他们的常设剧场里兴盛起来","role":"（第一个并列分句（主干主谓+地点状语））","note":"难点： companies 熟词僻义『剧团』+ permanent theaters『常设剧场』 company 此处不是『公司』，戏剧语境中 the professional companies 指『职业（演出）剧团／戏班』；their 回指 companies；permanent theaters 与上文临时演出相对，指『固定的／常设的剧场』（呼应 the first public playhouse was built）。prospered 译『兴盛、繁荣』。"},{"en":"and university men with literary ambitions","lit":"怀有文学抱负的大学才子们","role":"（第二个并列分句的主语（with 介词短语作定语））","note":"难点： university men 译『大学才子／受过大学教育的人』+ with 短语后置定语前移 university men 不是泛泛『大学里的男人』，指『受过大学教育、有学问的人（大学才子）』；with literary ambitions 是后置定语修饰 university men，汉语提前译『怀有文学抱负的（大学才子）』。"},{"en":"were quick to turn to these theaters","lit":"很快转向这些剧场","role":"（第二分句谓语）","note":"难点： be quick to do 译『迅速／急于做』+ these theaters 回指常设剧场 be quick to do 不是『在剧场里很快』，固定结构意为『迅速地、急于（做某事）』；turn to 此处是『转向、投身于』；these theaters 回指上文 their permanent theaters，即那些常设剧场，须还原指代。"},{"en":"as offering a means of livelihood.","lit":"因为（这些剧场）提供了一种谋生手段","role":"（as 引导的方式/原因状语（非谓语 offering））","note":"难点： as offering 表原因『因其提供』+ a means of livelihood 固定搭配 as + 现在分词 offering 在此表原因／看待方式，意为『把剧场看作（因其）提供……』，可译『因为它们提供了……』；a means of livelihood 是固定搭配，means 此处是单复同形的『手段、方法』，译『一种谋生（的）手段』，不要把 means 误当复数或译『意义』。"}]},{"n":49,"en":"A native literary drama had been created, its alliance with the public playhouses established, and at least some of its great traditions had been begun.","refZh":"一种本土的文学戏剧已经诞生，它与公共剧场的结合也已建立，而且它至少有一些伟大的传统已经开创。","analysis":"","chunks":[{"en":"A native literary drama had been created,","lit":"一种本土的文学戏剧已经被创造出来","role":"（第一个并列分句（被动语态主干））","note":"难点： native 译『本土的』+ had been created 被动转主动/无主句 native 此处指『本国的、本土的』（与上文从外国引入的 classical 古典剧相对）；had been created 是被动语态，汉语可转主动或无主句『（一种本土文学戏剧）已经诞生／已经形成』，避免『被创造』的翻译腔。这是后两个分句两个 its 的回指对象，须记牢。"},{"en":"its alliance with the public playhouses established,","lit":"它与公共剧场的同盟已经建立","role":"（第二个并列分句（独立主格结构，省略 had been））","note":"难点： its 回指 native literary drama + alliance 译『结合/联盟』+ 省略 had been its 回指首句 A native literary drama，译『这种戏剧的』；alliance 不一定译『军事同盟』，此处指戏剧与剧场的『结合、联系、联盟』；本分句是独立主格，established 前省略了 had been（承前），补全为 its alliance… had been established，译『（这种戏剧）与公共剧场的结合也已建立』。"},{"en":"and at least some of its great traditions had been begun.","lit":"并且至少它的一些伟大传统已经开始","role":"（第三个并列分句（被动语态））","note":"难点： its 再次回指该戏剧 + at least some of 程度范围词 + had been begun 被动 第二个 its 仍回指 A native literary drama；at least some of 是程度／范围限定『至少一部分』，不可漏译或译成『所有』（A7 程度范围词失察）；its great traditions 译『它的一些伟大传统』，had been begun 被动转主动『已经开创／已经奠定』。"}]},{"n":50,"en":"To realize how great was the dramatic activity, we must remember further that hosts of plays have been lost, and that probably there is no author of note whose entire work has survived.","refZh":"要想认识到当时的戏剧活动多么繁荣，我们还必须记住：大量剧本已经失传，而且很可能没有哪一位知名作家的全部作品能够完整地流传至今。","analysis":"","chunks":[{"en":"To realize how great was the dramatic activity,","lit":"要认识到戏剧活动有多么繁荣","role":"（目的状语（to do）+ 内含感叹/倒装宾语从句）","note":"难点： To realize 目的状语提前 + how great was… 倒装 + dramatic activity 术语 句首 To realize… 是目的状语，译『要（想）认识到……』；how great was the dramatic activity 是 realize 的宾语从句，how great 提前引起的倒装，还原语序为 how great the dramatic activity was，译『戏剧活动（创作）有多么繁荣／空前活跃』；dramatic activity 承上文 the dramatic activity，统一术语。"},{"en":"we must remember further that hosts of plays have been lost,","lit":"我们必须进一步记住，大量剧本已经失传","role":"（主干主谓 + 第一个 that 宾语从句）","note":"难点： hosts of 熟词僻义『大量的』+ further 译『进一步』+ have been lost『失传』 hosts of 不是『主人们』，host of / hosts of 是固定搭配『大量的、许多的』；further 此处是『进一步、还要』；have been lost 被动，结合 plays（剧本）语境译『已经失传／散佚』，比『被丢失』地道。"},{"en":"and that probably there is no author of note","lit":"而且大概没有哪个知名作家","role":"（第二个 that 宾语从句（与前并列）的主干）","note":"难点： of note 熟词僻义『有名的』+ no…（双重否定铺垫） and that 与前一个 that 并列、同作 remember 的宾语；author of note 中 of note 是固定短语『有名的、知名的』（note 此处=名声），译『知名作家／有名望的作家』，不是『笔记』；probably 译『大概、很可能』；no author… 与下文 whose entire work has survived 配合构成『没有谁……是完整保存的』的含蓄否定。"},{"en":"whose entire work has survived.","lit":"其全部作品都流传了下来","role":"（whose 后置定语从句（修饰 author））","note":"难点： whose 指 author + survive 熟词僻义『留存/流传至今』+ 与 no 合成『没有谁的全部作品都留存』 whose 的先行词是 author of note；survive 此处不是『幸存（于灾难）』，而是『（作品）留存、流传至今』；no author… whose entire work has survived 直译『没有作家其全部作品都留存了』，即『几乎没有哪位名家的作品能完整流传下来』，这一含蓄否定要译出『几乎没有……是完整的』的意味。"}]}]},{"id":"section3-part-a","kind":"writing","title":"Section III Part A 写作","directions":"51. Directions: Write an email to all international experts on campus inviting them to attend the graduation ceremony. In your email you should include time, place and other relevant information about the ceremony. You should write about 100 words neatly on the ANSWER SHEET. Do not use your own name at the end of the email. Use “Li Ming” instead. (10 points)","prompt":"51. Directions: Write an email to all international experts on campus inviting them to attend the graduation ceremony. In your email you should include time, place and other relevant information about the ceremony. You should write about 100 words neatly on the ANSWER SHEET. Do not use your own name at the end of the email. Use “Li Ming” instead. (10 points)","points":10,"image":null,"model":{"essay":"Dear experts, On behalf of our university, I am writing to cordially invite you, our respected international experts, to attend the graduation ceremony of this year's graduates. The ceremony will be held at 9:00 a.m. on June 28th in the main auditorium of our campus. It will feature the conferring of degrees and speeches from professors and student representatives. Formal dress is recommended, and please arrive fifteen minutes early to be seated. We would be truly honored to have you share this memorable occasion with us. Your presence would mean a great deal to our graduates. Thank you for your continued support. Yours sincerely, Li Ming","translation":"尊敬的各位专家：\n谨代表我校，我写信诚挚邀请各位尊敬的外籍专家出席今年毕业生的毕业典礼。\n典礼将于 6 月 28 日上午 9 点在我校主礼堂举行。典礼内容包括授予学位以及教授和学生代表致辞。建议着正装，并请提前十五分钟入座。\n若能有各位与我们共度这一难忘时刻，我们将深感荣幸。您的到场对我们的毕业生意义非凡。感谢各位一贯的支持。\n此致 李明","breakdown":[{"tag":"【第 1 段】","core":"首段亮身份表来意","text":"【第 1 段】 首段亮身份表来意 。用 On behalf of our university, I am writing to cordially invite you... 落实“开门见山表来意”：一句点出校方身份并直陈邀请目的，完成 outline 第一段“称呼＋写信目的”的功能，正式敬语 cordially invite 也定住了语域。"},{"tag":"【第 2 段】","core":"要点段逐项落实","text":"【第 2 段】 要点段逐项落实 。held at 9:00 a.m. on June 28th 落时间，in the main auditorium 落地点，conferring of degrees and speeches 与 Formal dress is recommended, and please arrive fifteen minutes early 落其他信息——正是“细节落地不空喊”，把三个须覆盖要点全部数得清。"},{"tag":"【第 3 段】","core":"礼貌收尾正确署名","text":"【第 3 段】 礼貌收尾正确署名 。We would be truly honored... 表期待、Thank you for your continued support 致谢，末尾 Yours sincerely, Li Ming 完成“礼貌收束再落款”，且严格用了 Li Ming、没写真名，守住本题最致命的署名硬约束。"}],"wordCount":106}},{"id":"section3-part-b","kind":"writing","title":"Section III Part B 写作","directions":"52. Directions: Write an essay of 160-200 words based on the picture below. In your essay, you should 1)describe the picture briefly, 2)interpret the meaning, and 3)give your comments. You should write neatly on the ANSWER SHEET.（20 points）","prompt":"52. Directions: Write an essay of 160-200 words based on the picture below. In your essay, you should 1)describe the picture briefly, 2)interpret the meaning, and 3)give your comments. You should write neatly on the ANSWER SHEET.（20 points）","points":10,"image":{"src":"assets/writing/2018-english-one-section3-part-b.jpg","alt":"图片中一位男士坐在办公桌前，面前摆放着一台电脑","width":"1024","height":"1024","local":"assets/writing/2018-english-one-section3-part-b.jpg"},"model":{"essay":"As is vividly depicted in the drawing, a student sits before a computer whose screen displays a course-selection system. Pointing his finger at the screen, he is caught between two voices: the left bubble praises courses that are innovative, cutting-edge yet demanding, while the right one favours those offering high marks, easy passes and little homework. Simple as it seems, the picture conveys a thought-provoking message about how many college students make choices today. Rather than asking what a course can truly teach them, they weigh only how effortlessly they can earn a good grade. This utilitarian mindset, if left unchecked, gradually turns learning from a pursuit of knowledge into a hunt for the easiest reward, hollowing out the very purpose of higher education. In my view, we should firmly resist such short-sighted temptation. A challenging course may cost more time and effort, but it is precisely difficulty that sharpens our thinking and prepares us for real problems ahead. Therefore, both students and universities ought to value genuine growth over comfortable grades, so that education can once again nourish minds rather than merely decorate transcripts.","translation":"正如漫画生动描绘的那样，一名学生坐在电脑前，屏幕上显示着选课系统。他用手指指着屏幕，被两种声音拉扯着：左边的对话框推崇那些知识新颖、注重创新却有难度的课程，右边的则青睐给分高、容易通过、作业又少的课程。\n画面虽然简单，却传递出一个发人深省的信息，折射出如今许多大学生是怎样做选择的。他们不去问一门课究竟能教会自己什么，而只掂量能多轻松地拿到好分数。这种功利心态若不加遏制，会渐渐把学习从对知识的追求变成对最省力回报的追逐，掏空高等教育本来的意义。\n在我看来，我们应当坚决抵制这种短视的诱惑。一门有难度的课或许要花更多时间和精力，但恰恰是难度磨砺了我们的思维，让我们为将来真正的问题做好准备。因此，学生和高校都应当把真实的成长看得比舒适的高分更重，好让教育重新滋养心智，而不只是装点成绩单。","breakdown":[{"tag":"【第 1 段】","core":"描述段落实客观描述","text":"【第 1 段】 描述段落实客观描述 。开头 As is vividly depicted 客观入题，随后 he is caught between two voices 把左右对话框的对立摆出来，全段只描述不评论，正是第一段要承担的功能，也用上了『客观描述入题』与『呈现画面冲突』两个句架。"},{"tag":"【第 2 段】","core":"释义段升华为社会现象","text":"【第 2 段】 释义段升华为社会现象 。用 Simple as it seems, the picture conveys a thought-provoking message 由画转意，再以 Rather than asking what a course can truly teach them 点出功利心态，把具体画面升华成一种普遍现象，完成寓意引申。"},{"tag":"【第 3 段】","core":"评论段表态并呼吁","text":"【第 3 段】 评论段表态并呼吁 。In my view, we should firmly resist 旗帜鲜明表态，precisely difficulty that sharpens our thinking 给出理由，末句 both students and universities ought to value genuine growth 以呼吁收尾，回扣第二段寓意。"}],"wordCount":185}}],"questionCount":45,"objectiveCount":45,"answerTable":{"ans-section1":"完形填空（四选一） Section I （1–20 题） 1 C for · 2 A faith · 3 D price · 4 B Then · 5 D When · 6 B produces · 7 C connect · 8 D to · 9 B mood · 10 A counterparts · 11 B Lucky · 12 B protect · 13 A between · 14 C introduced · 15 D inside · 16 A discovered · 17 C fooled · 18 B willing · 19 A In contrast · 20 C unreliable \n（逐题解析见 完形填空（四选一）解析页\n）","ans-section2-part-a-1":"阅读理解（四选一） Section II Part A (1) （21–25 题） 21 D · 22 C · 23 A · 24 D · 25 B \n（逐题解析见 阅读理解（四选一）解析页\n）","ans-section2-part-a-2":"阅读理解（四选一） Section II Part A (2) （26–30 题） 26 D · 27 A · 28 B · 29 C · 30 A \n（逐题解析见 阅读理解（四选一）解析页\n）","ans-section2-part-a-3":"阅读理解（四选一） Section II Part A (3) （31–35 题） 31 B · 32 C · 33 D · 34 D · 35 B \n（逐题解析见 阅读理解（四选一）解析页\n）","ans-section2-part-a-4":"阅读理解（四选一） Section II Part A (4) （36–40 题） 36 B · 37 A · 38 A · 39 C · 40 D \n（逐题解析见 阅读理解（四选一）解析页\n）","ans-section2-part-b":"段落排序 Section II Part B 41=E → 预给C → 42=G → 43=A → 预给F → 44=B → 45=D \n（逐题解析见 段落排序解析页\n）","ans-section2-part-c":"英译汉（划线句子翻译） Section II Part C \n主观题 · 参考范文与逐题解析见\n 英译汉（划线句子翻译）解析页","ans-section3-part-a":"写作 Section III Part A \n主观题 · 参考范文与逐题解析见\n 写作解析页","ans-section3-part-b":"写作 Section III Part B \n主观题 · 参考范文与逐题解析见\n 写作解析页"},"coverage":{"objective":45,"explained":40,"missingSections":[]}};
